To comply with the EU's Carbon Border Adjustment Mechanism (CBAM), importers must obtain a CBAM Authorisation. This authorisation is essential for importing regulated goods into the EU, ensuring that the carbon reporting and payment obligations set by the EU to reduce carbon leakage are met.
The Authorisation Management Module allows to request a CBAM Authorisation and manage all related tasks. It serves as entry point to initiate the process, track the authorisation application status, and handle the ongoing authorisation.
The Authorisation management module includes the following processes:
• Authorisation granting;
• Amendment;
• Revocation;
• End revocation;
• Re-assessment;
• Information which may affect the status or content of the decision;
• Right to appeal;
• Request for additional information;
• Guarantee registration;
• Guarantee adjustment;
• Guarantee release.

DRMC includes the following functionalities:
• View CBAM declarant account;
• View quantifiable assets overview;
• View financial liabilities;
• Monitor quantifiable assets:
o Query goods and emissions;
o Query coverage;
o Query certificates;
o Monitor CBAM declaration;
• Control quantifiable assets
o View emission coverage rule compliance control results;
The quantifiable assets are based on data from different CBAM components and include:
• Goods quantities (in tonnes (t) and megawatt-hours (MWh));
• Emissions (in tonnes of CO₂ (tCO₂));
• CBAM certificates (in number of certificates and in euros (€)).
The purchase of CBAM certificates and all related processes will only begin on 1 February 2027. In addition, the first CBAM declaration will be submitted during 2027.
As a result, the screens related to CBAM certificates and CBAM declarations will not contain any data at this stage.

